  <article class="prose">
    <section>
      <h2>The Problem: The Bloat of Modern API Workbenches</h2>
      <p>
        Modern API clients that began as lightweight testing utilities have transformed into multi-gigabyte Electron monoliths requiring mandatory team accounts, cloud syncing, and constant update prompts. For engineers debugging endpoints or working in air-gapped/secure environments, transmitting authorization tokens and internal JSON payloads through third-party servers presents severe compliance risks.
      </p>
      <p>
        Postie was designed as an intentional counterweight: a single-tab browser tool that requires zero installation, works offline, and keeps all sensitive request credentials strictly inside the local browser sandbox.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Architecture &amp; Web Standards Implementation</h2>
      <p>
        Postie is engineered without frameworks, bundlers, or third-party CSS libraries, achieving maximum performance and longevity:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Native Web Fetch API:</strong> Dispatches HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, PATCH) directly from the client window, honoring standard CORS policies and browser security models.</li>
        <li><strong>Header &amp; Status Inspection:</strong> Parses HTTP response status codes, timing metrics (DNS to last byte), and response headers into a readable diagnostic grid.</li>
        <li><strong>Safe Syntax Highlighting:</strong> Formats raw JSON responses with an in-memory lexical tokenizer written in vanilla JavaScript, avoiding heavy highlight libraries like Prism or Highlight.js.</li>
        <li><strong>LocalStorage Workspace Persistence:</strong> Saves request tabs, bearer tokens, headers, and historical requests locally without transmitting data to external servers.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>CORS Handling &amp; Security Model</h2>
      <p>
        Because Postie runs directly in the browser, all network requests are subject to standard CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) headers. For development APIs, local endpoints (localhost:3000, 8080), and public open APIs with <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *</code>, requests execute with zero configuration. For production APIs requiring restricted origins, Postie provides explicit diagnostic feedback explaining which preflight headers failed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Postie vs Heavy Desktop Clients</h2>
      <table class="comparison-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Dimension</th>
            <th scope="col">Traditional Desktop Clients (Postman / Insomnia)</th>
            <th scope="col">Postie Zero-Dependency Workbench</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Installation &amp; Disk</th>
            <td>500MB+ Electron binary installation and frequent updater dialogs</td>
